","nome":"German","termine":"Schuppen","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"yi","nome":"Yiddish","termine":"שאָפּע","traslitterazione":"shope","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"Uncertain. * Doroszewski suggests an association with English shoplifting. * Bańko suggests that the word may have either derived from German Schuppen or Yiddish שאָפּע (shope) which later, due to the massive migration of Jewish diaspora to the United States in the last XIX century, may have overlap"},{"parola":"żłób","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"calco","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"more than one origin proposed","significato":"trough (elongated container located in a stable or barn into which animal food is placed)","termine_origine":"זשלאָב","termine_origine_traslitterazione":"zhlob","catena":[{"codice":"yi","nome":"Yiddish","termine":"זשלאָב","traslitterazione":"zhlob","categoria":"calco"}],"lontana":"yi","ambiti":["finanza","scienza","diritto_politica","agricoltura"],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"yi","nome":"Yiddish","termine":"זשלאָב","traslitterazione":"zhlob","categoria":"calco"}],"testo":"Inherited from Old Polish żłób. Etymology 1, noun 2 sense 1 is a semantic loan from Yiddish זשלאָב (zhlob, “redneck, bumpkin”). Doublet of żleb (“couloir”), a borrowing from Slovak."}]}